WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.640
Authorization decides whether an
authenticated caller may perform an action on a

2
00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:08.651
resource.

3
00:00:08.731 --> 00:00:16.284
In APIs, object-level authorization is
especially important because many endpoints

4
00:00:16.364 --> 00:00:18.848
accept direct identifiers.

5
00:00:18.928 --> 00:00:25.964
A caller may be allowed to view their own
order, but not another customer's order.

6
00:00:26.044 --> 00:00:32.873
They may be allowed to edit a project they
own, but not one in a different tenant.

7
00:00:32.953 --> 00:00:38.230
Every sensitive object access should be
checked in context.

8
00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:45.346
Function-level authorization controls
which operations a caller may perform.

9
00:00:45.426 --> 00:00:50.496
A user who can view an account may not be
allowed to close it.

10
00:00:50.576 --> 00:00:56.992
A support operator may reset a password
but not change billing ownership.

11
00:00:57.072 --> 00:01:02.349
A service account may read inventory but
not update pricing.

12
00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:07.913
Role-based access control, attribute-based
access control,

13
00:01:07.993 --> 00:01:15.236
relationship-based rules, and policy
engines can all help when they are applied at

14
00:01:15.316 --> 00:01:19.972
the operation that actually changes or
reveals data.

15
00:01:20.052 --> 00:01:24.191
Tenant isolation deserves special
attention.

16
00:01:24.271 --> 00:01:31.514
Multi-tenant APIs must ensure that tenant
context cannot be chosen casually by the

17
00:01:31.594 --> 00:01:32.318
client.

18
00:01:32.398 --> 00:01:36.020
A tenant ID in a header, path, token
claim,

19
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:43.757
or request body should be validated
against trusted server-side authorization state.

20
00:01:43.837 --> 00:01:50.045
Weak tenant checks can expose one
customer's data to another customer,

21
00:01:50.125 --> 00:01:54.781
even when authentication appears to work
correctly.

22
00:01:54.861 --> 00:02:00.552
Avoid relying on hidden UI controls or
client-side enforcement.

23
00:02:00.632 --> 00:02:04.668
Attackers and testers can call APIs
directly.

24
00:02:04.748 --> 00:02:07.335
Mobile apps can be inspected.

25
00:02:07.415 --> 00:02:10.415
Browser requests can be modified.

26
00:02:10.495 --> 00:02:13.186
Internal clients can be buggy.

27
00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:19.681
The backend should enforce authorization
at every sensitive operation,

28
00:02:19.761 --> 00:02:26.590
using least-privileged service permissions
and explicit ownership checks.

29
00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:31.223
If an API endpoint can affect data or
business state,

30
00:02:31.303 --> 00:02:36.580
the server must decide whether the caller
is allowed to do it.
